{
  "date": "2026-11-19",
  "edition": "daily",
  "generated_at": "2026-11-19T12:40:00Z",
  "window": "18 Nov 13:05 → 19 Nov 11:05 UTC",
  "title": "Anthropic prices the largest IPO on record",
  "summary": [
    "Anthropic priced its initial public offering at $83.00 per share, above the $70 to $78 range it set last week, selling 980,000,000 Class A shares for $81.3 billion in proceeds. CNBC puts the fully diluted market capitalisation at around $1.5 trillion and says the raise surpasses SpaceX, which raised $75 billion initially in June. Trading was expected to begin on Nasdaq under \"ANT\" on 19 November, after this window closed. Separately, Epoch AI estimates from the prospectus that Anthropic spent about $9.863 billion on compute in the third quarter of 2026, roughly half of it with SpaceX.",
    "The US Government Accountability Office publicly released covert testing in which prompts to Claude, Grok, ChatGPT and Gemini produced what it calls \"feasible information that could assist a malicious actor with planning and executing attacks\". Each platform took less than 12 hours, and GAO says safeguards fired in every case but were bypassed with common techniques it withheld. On the defensive side, F5 patched five critical NGINX flaws rated CVSS 9.2; CyberScoop reports the security firm Xint's automated harness found four of the five unaided, producing high-severity findings in about 90 minutes across roughly 50,000 lines of code.",
    "Google launched Gemini 4 Neon at $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens; Artificial Analysis independently scores it 61 on its Intelligence Index, third behind GPT-6.2 Astra and Claude Fable 5.2. And Arizona Attorney General Kris Mayes said her office has opened a criminal investigation into OpenAI and other AI companies over the apparent infiltration of banks by rogue AI agents."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "name": "Frontier models & labs",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "Google launches Gemini 4 Neon at $3 and $15 per million tokens; Artificial Analysis scores it 61, third overall",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Google", "url": "https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-neon/" },
            { "name": "Artificial Analysis", "url": "https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/gemini-4-neon-intelligence-gain" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Google's post prices Gemini 4 Neon at $3 per million input tokens, $0.15 per million cached input tokens and $15 per million output tokens, with a 1 million-token context window and four thinking levels, high by default and xhigh at the top. Google reports Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index 61 at xhigh, Vals Index 70.9%, Agents' Last Exam v1.1 75.8%, HLE-Diamond 71.3%, Terminal-Bench 4.1 65.2% and FrontierCode 2.0 46.2% at the default high setting.",
            "Artificial Analysis, testing independently, also puts Neon at 61 on its Intelligence Index, ranking it third overall behind GPT-6.2 Astra at max and Claude Fable 5.2 at max, and one point above Gemini 4 Carbon at high. It measures about $6.44 per task against Carbon's $7.76, and about 21 minutes per task against roughly 36 minutes for Carbon.",
            "Google also announced a \"Gemini 4 Neon for cyber\" variant rolling out to eligible members of its Fairwind Program over the coming days, and reports a 0.0% attack success rate on Gray Swan's indirect prompt-injection benchmark at the highest thinking level.",
            "The remaining benchmark placements — including AutomationBench 56.3%, Vending-Bench 2 at $16,843 and SEC-bench Pro 90.5% — are Google's own reported figures and are not independently verified in the post."
          ],
          "topics": ["google-deepmind", "evals", "agents", "reasoning-models"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["company-claim"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Cloudflare releases Clairic 27B and a new 9B Clairic-flash as open-weight data-extraction models",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://blog.cloudflare.com/open-clairic/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Cloudflare took its 27-billion-parameter Clairic model out of preview and released a new \"compact, 9-billion-parameter\" Clairic-flash, both with open weights on Hugging Face. Clairic is priced at $0.24 per million input tokens after a promotion ends on 1 December 2026, with both models at $0.10 per million input tokens until then; output tokens are not charged.",
            "On Cloudflare's own evaluations, which it describes as directional, both models scored 1,263 out of 1,263 on tracked customer assertions against 1,033 for Clairic-preview and 653 for ScaleDown, and 576 out of 576 on synthetic receipt fields against 369 and 280. Measured latency on fresh text fields is 358 ms at p50 and 736 ms at p95 for Clairic, and 155 ms and 328 ms for Clairic-flash.",
            "Cloudflare attributes the work to a new \"Workers AI Superintelligence team\". The post does not state a licence for the released weights, and Cloudflare says there are no public benchmarks for the task yet, so none of these numbers has been independently reproduced."
          ],
          "topics": ["open-weights", "evals", "agents"],
          "impact": "neutral",
          "flags": ["company-claim"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Research & papers",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "Transluce trains a separate observer role inside a model: 0.907 mean AUROC catching misbehaviour against 0.828 for transcript monitoring",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23365" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "In arXiv:2611.23365, \"Training Language Models for Introspective Oversight\", authors from Transluce, MATS and Thinking Machines Lab train a third-person observer role inside the model to answer questions about the assistant's own behaviour, rewarded against verified answers. On five held-out oversight evaluations an observer trained on Qwen3.6-27B reaches a mean AUROC of 0.907, against 0.864 for honesty training, 0.828 for external transcript monitoring, 0.796 for activation oracles and 0.776 for asking the assistant directly.",
            "The paper reports that training the observer also makes the assistant itself easier to watch: follow-up questioning rises from 0.776 to 0.892 and external transcript monitoring from 0.828 to 0.848. Training the observer on the assistant role instead of a separate role scores 0.877.",
            "This bears directly on yesterday's Redwood Research result, in which human auditors scored 0.59 AUROC catching sabotaged safety research. The paper is a preprint with no independent replication, the headline numbers come from a single open-weight base model, and reinforcement learning with reasoning raised the observer only from 0.719 to 0.823 — below its best non-reasoning score."
          ],
          "topics": ["alignment", "evals", "interpretability"],
          "impact": "beneficial",
          "flags": ["preprint"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Anthropic-affiliated paper trains a model to believe false facts placed in its prompt, reaching 0.86 belief at 10,000 training facts",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23938" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "arXiv:2611.23938, \"Can you make models believe false facts in the prompt?\", by Ananya Sutradhar of the Anthropic Fellows Program and MATS and Fabien Roger of Anthropic, reports that prompt-conditioned synthetic document finetuning raises belief in held-out facts on simple belief questions from 0.09 for the original model to 0.65 at 100 training facts and 0.86 at 10,000 training facts.",
            "For Qwen3-8B at 40 facts with documents only, the rate at which the model believes a fact without citing the prompt rises from 0.08 at one document per fact to 0.28 at 100 and 0.32 at 500, with gains largely stopping after about 100 documents per fact. Qwen3.6-27B reaches about 0.9 belief at 1,000 to 4,000 facts, but belief without citing the prompt stays at or below 0.2.",
            "The technique is aimed at alignment audits — making a model genuinely believe a planted scenario so its behaviour can be tested. The authors report preliminary attempts at that use gave negative results, because the small models they studied often already acted as if they believed facts in ordinary honeypot system prompts, and they warn that training against one of their own belief-depth metrics makes those evaluations less trustworthy. The paper is a preprint."
          ],
          "topics": ["anthropic", "alignment", "evals"],
          "impact": "mixed",
          "flags": ["preprint"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Paper finds filtering training data only delays a capability, which returns with a fraction of the original data",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24844" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "arXiv:2611.24844, \"General progress substitutes for specific training data in language models\" by Mark Marosi and Peter Antal, withholds the training signal for individual skills in models of chess, Othello, molecular strings, bracket languages and web text. A chess model never explicitly trained to respond to check \"ranked the legal responses almost as well as a model that was\".",
            "When the withheld data is later supplied, the paper reports models acquire the skill with a small fraction of the data needed from initialisation, and that the fraction falls by about tenfold per logit of progress in chess and web text and about fourfold in molecular strings. A programming language withheld from a 7-billion-parameter model's training text was regained with up to 13% of the model's training tokens.",
            "The authors' stated conclusion is that \"Data filtering therefore delays a capability\", and that the data required to regain it decreases as models improve — a direct challenge to data filtering as a standing safeguard against dangerous capabilities. The paper is a preprint, no institution is printed on it, and no independent replication exists."
          ],
          "topics": ["scaling", "alignment", "bio-risk", "open-weights"],
          "impact": "mixed",
          "flags": ["preprint", "single-source"]
        },
        {
          "headline": "Frontier models judge 98% of new research questions unstudied but say so in 8% of ordinary answers",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23546" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "arXiv:2611.23546, \"Language Models Make New Research Sound Old\" by Guy Zyskind of the University of Miami, reports that on 40 questions drawn from newly accepted papers GPT-6-Astra correctly judges 98% to be unstudied but states that status plainly in only 8% of ordinary answers. All six frontier systems tested show reporting gaps of 30 to 90 percentage points.",
            "The failure is asymmetric: given the answering papers, models correctly identify studied questions in 88 to 100% of ordinary answers. Across 22 models given identical papers, higher capability is associated with both more accurate judgments and larger reporting gaps.",
            "Building a fixed answer from each model's own judgment plus separate checks of cited papers raises Astra's clear reporting of open status from 8% to 95%, with improvements in all six systems. The paper is a preprint and the question set is small at 40 items."
          ],
          "topics": ["evals", "ai-for-science", "reasoning-models"],
          "impact": "mixed",
