        {
          "headline": "Attack recovers other clients' training sentences verbatim from federated learning, up to 80.2% of them at 100% precision",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24689" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "arXiv:2611.24689, \"Stealing Exact Training Data from Other Clients during Federated Learning\", reports what the authors call the first exact sentence recovery by a malicious client against FedAvg aggregates, at 100% precision, using a loop that subtracts the gradient contributions of already-recovered sentences from the aggregate to expose the rest.",
            "On aggregates containing up to four times more tokens than the model's hidden dimension, the attack recovers up to 80.2% of client sentences exactly — a regime in which, the abstract says, existing attacks recover none exactly.",
            "Federated learning is sold on the premise that raw data never leaves the client. The paper is a preprint with no independent replication, no affiliations are rendered on the arXiv page, and it reports results against FedAvg rather than against deployed systems with secure aggregation or differential privacy."
          ],
          "topics": ["privacy", "cyber-offense", "agent-security"],
