          "headline": "US-China commission asks Congress to bar Chinese open-weight models from critical infrastructure and national security systems",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "USCC", "url": "https://www.uscc.gov/annual-report/2026-annual-report-congress" },
            { "name": "USCC recommendations", "url": "https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2026-11/Comprehensive_List_of_2026_Recommendations.pdf" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission's 2026 Annual Report to Congress, released on 18 November, includes Chapter 8, \"Silicon to Sovereignty: China's Bid to Control the AI Stack from Wafer to Weights, Part One\". Recommendation 18 asks Congress to require Commerce, within 180 days, to identify sensitive uses \"including systems controlling critical infrastructure, performing national security functions, or processing bulk sensitive personal data\" and \"prohibit new deployment of covered models in those uses absent a time-limited authorization based on independently evaluated safeguards\".",
            "Recommendation 19 would require federal agencies, and any state or local government or other entity receiving federal funds or tax credits, to disclose use of AI models developed by an entity headquartered in or organised under the laws of China, \"including models fine-tuned on or derived from them, wherever they are hosted\", and would have businesses disclose such use as a material business risk in SEC filings.",
            "Recommendation 20 would amend the Export Control Reform Act of 2018 to condition exports of advanced AI chips to China on exporter certification that they will not divert advanced semiconductor manufacturing or AI capacity from the US market, covering advanced-node wafer fabrication, high-bandwidth memory and advanced packaging, with criminal penalties for false certification and a 30-day congressional review before any waiver.",
            "These are recommendations to Congress, not law. The Commission has no rulemaking power, and its recommendations have historically been adopted selectively."
          ],
          "topics": ["china", "export-controls", "open-weights", "us-federal-policy"],
          "storylines": ["china-distillation-export-controls"],
