        {
          "headline": "Commerce rule restricting Chinese firms' remote access to advanced chips takes effect; TSMC and Samsung reported to pause some China shipments",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Federal Register", "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/11/19/2026-23529/addressing-remote-access-to-advanced-semiconductors" },
            { "name": "Newsquawk", "url": "https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/tsmc-2330-tt-and-samsung-electronics-005930-ks-suspend-shipments-of-certain-chips-to-chinese-customers-while-seeking-us-export-licences-under-new-restrictions-ft-reports" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "The Bureau of Industry and Security interim final rule \"Addressing Remote Access to Advanced Semiconductors\" was published in the Federal Register on 19 November and states \"This rule is effective November 19, 2026.\" It restricts exports, reexports and in-country transfers to overseas data centres that remotely supply significant volumes of compute to companies headquartered in Country Group D:5 or Macau, and adds a licence requirement for exports of advanced semiconductors to Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Brazil, plus any further country the Secretary of Commerce designates.",
            "The rule exempts semiconductors destined to companies headquartered or ultimately parented in the United States provided reporting requirements are met, says the licence requirement will not apply to commercial recommender systems such as those used by online retailers or social media companies, and formally rescinds the regulations implementing the Biden-era AI Diffusion Rule, which it says \"has never been enforced\".",
            "Newsquawk, reporting the Financial Times at 10:44 UTC on 19 November, says TSMC and Samsung Electronics have suspended shipments of certain chips to Chinese customers while seeking US export licences under the new restrictions.",
            "The rule's substance was announced on 16 November, outside this window; what is new is Federal Register publication and the rule taking effect. The FT report is the only source for the TSMC and Samsung pause, and the FT article itself could not be opened from here, so the detail available is limited to that one sentence — no product categories, customers or duration."
          ],
