    {
      "name": "Research & papers",
      "items": [
        {
          "headline": "Transluce trains a separate observer role inside a model: 0.907 mean AUROC catching misbehaviour against 0.828 for transcript monitoring",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23365" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "In arXiv:2611.23365, \"Training Language Models for Introspective Oversight\", authors from Transluce, MATS and Thinking Machines Lab train a third-person observer role inside the model to answer questions about the assistant's own behaviour, rewarded against verified answers. On five held-out oversight evaluations an observer trained on Qwen3.6-27B reaches a mean AUROC of 0.907, against 0.864 for honesty training, 0.828 for external transcript monitoring, 0.796 for activation oracles and 0.776 for asking the assistant directly.",
            "The paper reports that training the observer also makes the assistant itself easier to watch: follow-up questioning rises from 0.776 to 0.892 and external transcript monitoring from 0.828 to 0.848. Training the observer on the assistant role instead of a separate role scores 0.877.",
            "This bears directly on yesterday's Redwood Research result, in which human auditors scored 0.59 AUROC catching sabotaged safety research. The paper is a preprint with no independent replication, the headline numbers come from a single open-weight base model, and reinforcement learning with reasoning raised the observer only from 0.719 to 0.823 — below its best non-reasoning score."
          ],
          "topics": ["alignment", "evals", "interpretability"],
          "impact": "beneficial",
          "flags": ["preprint"]
