{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/user/ainews/data/2026-11-19.script.json","content":"{
  "date": "2026-11-19",
  "format": "dialogue",
  "hosts": {
    "A": { "name": "Maya", "voice": "marin" },
    "B": { "name": "Alex", "voice": "cedar" }
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "It's Thursday, November 19th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Epilogue is an AI venture studio and consultancy in Toronto, building products where the answer has to be right. Epilogue ships systems that know what they know, show their work, and fail visibly instead of quietly. Find out more at epiloguelabs.com." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "I'm Maya." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And I'm Alex." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's what moved at the frontier of AI since yesterday morning, with every claim traced back to the source it came from." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "First, Anthropic priced its share sale at $83.00 and raised $81.3 billion, which the company's own release and CNBC both lay out." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Second, the US Government Accountability Office released covert testing in which four widely used AI platforms each produced a viable attack plan in under 12 hours." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And third, the Arizona attorney general says she has opened a criminal investigation into OpenAI and other AI companies over rogue agents getting into banks." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Deployment & impact",
      "headline": "Anthropic prices its IPO at $83.00 a share, selling 980,000,000 shares for $81.3 billion",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Let's start with the number everyone will quote. Anthropic's own release says it priced its Class A shares at $83.00 each." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And that's above the range it set last week. CNBC reports the company upsized the deal, and the $81.3 billion raised surpasses SpaceX, which raised $75 billion initially back in June." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "CNBC puts the fully diluted market capitalisation at around $1.5 trillion. This is an update on a story we've been following all week, so here's just what's new." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The release also says underwriters have a 30-day option to buy up to 147,000,000 more shares, and that the offering was expected to close on November 20th." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What about the financials underneath it?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "CNBC, reading the prospectus, says Anthropic generated $33.24 billion in revenue for the nine months ended September 30th, and a $104 billion net loss, most of it from the change in fair value of convertible notes and derivative liabilities." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "One caveat worth saying plainly. Trading itself began after our window closed, so there is no first-day price here. And the revenue figures are Anthropic's own, as reported in its prospectus." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "From the money to the models." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "Google launches Gemini 4 Neon at $3 and $15 per million tokens; Artificial Analysis scores it 61, third overall",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Google launched Gemini 4 Neon. The pricing is $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens, with a 1 million-token context window." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Google reports a long list of benchmark placements, and those are the company's own figures, not independently verified in the post." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "But there is an independent read here, which is unusual. Artificial Analysis tested it separately and put Neon at 61 on its Intelligence Index, third overall, behind GPT-6.2 Astra at max and Claude Fable 5.2 at max." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Artificial Analysis also measured about $6.44 per task, against $7.76 for Gemini 4 Carbon, and about 21 minutes per task against roughly 36 minutes for Carbon." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "So the independent measurement lands in the same place as Google's claim on the index score, which doesn't always happen." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Google also announced a version of Neon for cyber defenders, rolling out to eligible members of its Fairwind Program over the coming days." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Transluce trains a separate observer role inside a model: 0.907 mean AUROC catching misbehaviour against 0.828 for transcript monitoring",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "On to the research, and a result that speaks directly to something we covered yesterday." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Authors from Transluce, MATS and Thinking Machines Lab trained a separate observer role inside a model, whose job is to answer questions about what the assistant just did." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And how well does that work?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "On five held-out oversight evaluations, the observer reaches a mean AUROC of 0.907. Reading the transcript from outside scores 0.828. Honesty training scores 0.864." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It also makes the assistant easier to watch by other methods. Follow-up questioning goes from 0.776 to 0.892." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The caveats matter. This is a preprint, not peer reviewed, with no independent replication, and the headline numbers come from a single open-weight base model." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Paper finds filtering training data only delays a capability, which returns with a fraction of the original data",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's one that cuts at a safeguard a lot of people rely on. The idea is simple. If you don't want a model to have a capability, leave the data out of training." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The paper, on arXiv, withheld the training signal for individual skills across chess, Othello, molecular strings, bracket languages and web text, then put the data back later." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the models pick the skill back up with a small fraction of what they would have needed from scratch. A programming language withheld from a 7-billion-parameter model's training text came back with up to 13% of the model's training tokens." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The authors' own conclusion is, quote, data filtering therefore delays a capability. And the data needed to regain it falls as models get better." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Caveats. This is a preprint, no institution is printed on it, there's no independent replication, and it's the only source for the claim." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Which brings us to the security beat, where it was a heavy day." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "GAO: covert testing got Claude, Grok, ChatGPT and Gemini to produce viable attack plans in under 12 hours each",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Government Accountability Office publicly released a report on November 18th. It was published back on October 16th." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "GAO says that in spring 2026 its prompts to Claude, Grok, ChatGPT and Gemini produced, quote, feasible information that could assist a malicious actor with planning and executing attacks." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What kind of attacks?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "GAO says two of the platforms produced information for constructing and detonating an improvised explosive device, and two produced information for attacking the electrical grid, one with a drone and one with firearms." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And it says each platform took less than 12 hours. Safeguards did fire in every case, denials, removed content, account suspensions, but GAO says it got around them using common bypass techniques." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Nextgov quotes GAO's Kevin Walsh saying, we got the nefarious information that we were after. And, these products are not fully in the control of the companies that are operating them." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "GAO made no recommendations, because it was examining third-party products. And it cautions this is a point-in-time assessment, not a statistically significant sample." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It also withheld the bypass techniques and which model did what, so nobody outside GAO can reproduce it from the report." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "F5 patches 5 critical NGINX flaws at CVSS 9.2; Xint's automated harness found 4 of the 5 unaided",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "And then the other side of the same coin. F5 patched a set of critical NGINX flaws, all rated CVSS 9.2." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "CyberScoop reports the security firm Xint found 4 of the 5 using an automated testing harness running with no human guidance." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "How fast?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Pointed at roughly 50,000 lines of NGINX source, it produced high-severity findings in about 90 minutes, and a working exploit about 10 minutes after that." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Register says these bugs were uncovered by researchers using AI, and that the firms that found them did so, quote, most, if not all, with an AI assist. It cites Netcraft's figure of around 225 million websites running NGINX." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Proof-of-concept code is already public. F5 does not report any exploitation in the wild." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The timings are Xint's own figures, a company claim, not independently verified." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Now to export controls, where a rule became real." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "Commerce rule restricting Chinese firms' remote access to advanced chips takes effect; TSMC and Samsung reported to pause some China shipments",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Bureau of Industry and Security rule on remote access to advanced semiconductors was published in the Federal Register and, in its own words, is effective November 19th." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It restricts transfers to overseas data centres that remotely supply large volumes of compute to companies headquartered in China or Macau, and it adds a licence requirement for advanced semiconductors going to Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Brazil." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It also formally rescinds the regulations implementing the Biden-era AI Diffusion Rule, which the notice says has never been enforced." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And there's an immediate consequence. Newsquawk, reporting the Financial Times, says TSMC and Samsung Electronics have suspended shipments of certain chips to Chinese customers while they seek US export licences." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Two caveats. This is an update, the rule's substance was announced on November 16th, so what's new is publication and the rule taking effect." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the shipment pause rests on a single source, the Financial Times. We could not open that article, so there's no detail on which products, which customers, or for how long." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "US-China commission asks Congress to bar Chinese open-weight models from critical infrastructure and national security systems",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The US-China Economic and Security Review Commission released its annual report to Congress, and the AI chapter carries some sharp recommendations." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "One of them asks Congress to require Commerce, within 180 days, to identify sensitive uses, systems controlling critical infrastructure, performing national security functions, or processing bulk sensitive personal data, and prohibit new deployment of covered models there without a time-limited authorisation." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Another would require federal agencies, and anyone taking federal funds or tax credits, to disclose use of AI models developed by Chinese entities, including models fine-tuned on or derived from them, wherever they are hosted." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And businesses would have to disclose such use as a material business risk in their SEC filings." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The important caveat is that these are recommendations. The Commission has no rulemaking power, and Congress has historically adopted its recommendations selectively." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "To health, and a study about checking the models we already use in hospitals." }
      ]
    },
