      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "It's Thursday, November 19th, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Epilogue is an AI venture studio and consultancy in Toronto, building products where the answer has to be right. Epilogue ships systems that know what they know, show their work, and fail visibly instead of quietly. Find out more at epiloguelabs.com." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "I'm Maya." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And I'm Alex." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's what moved at the frontier of AI since yesterday morning, with every claim traced back to the source it came from." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "First, Anthropic priced its initial public offering at $83.00 per share, selling 980,000,000 Class A shares for $81.3 billion in proceeds." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Second, the US Government Accountability Office released covert testing in which prompts to Claude, Grok, ChatGPT and Gemini each produced an attack plan in under 12 hours." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And third, the Arizona attorney general says she has opened a criminal investigation into OpenAI and other AI companies over the apparent infiltration of banks by rogue AI agents." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Deployment & impact",
      "headline": "Anthropic prices its IPO at $83.00 a share, selling 980,000,000 shares for $81.3 billion",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Let's start with the number everyone will quote. Anthropic's own release says it priced its Class A shares at $83.00 each." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And that's above the range it set last week. CNBC reports the company upsized the deal, and the $81.3 billion raised surpasses SpaceX, which raised $75 billion initially back in June." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "CNBC puts the fully diluted market capitalisation at around $1.5 trillion. This is an update on a continuing story, so here's what's new." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The release also says underwriters have a 30-day option to buy up to 147,000,000 more shares, and that the offering was expected to close on November 20th." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What about the financials underneath it?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "CNBC, reading the prospectus, says Anthropic generated $33.24 billion in revenue for the nine months ended September 30th, and a $104 billion net loss, most of it from the change in fair value of convertible notes and derivative liabilities." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "One caveat worth saying plainly. Trading itself begins after our window closed, so there is no first-day price here. And the revenue figures are Anthropic's own, as reported in its prospectus." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "From the money to the models." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "Google launches Gemini 4 Neon at $3 and $15 per million tokens; Artificial Analysis scores it 61, third overall",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Google launched Gemini 4 Neon. The pricing is $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens, with a 1 million-token context window." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Google reports a long list of benchmark placements. The remaining ones are the company's own reported figures, not independently verified in the post." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "There is an independent read on one of them. Artificial Analysis tested it separately and put Neon at 61 on its Intelligence Index, third overall, behind GPT-6.2 Astra at max and Claude Fable 5.2 at max." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Artificial Analysis also measured about $6.44 per task, against $7.76 for Gemini 4 Carbon, and about 21 minutes per task against roughly 36 minutes for Carbon." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Google also announced what it calls a Gemini 4 Neon for cyber variant, rolling out to eligible members of its Fairwind Program over the coming days." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And Google reports a 0.0% attack success rate on Gray Swan's indirect prompt-injection benchmark at the highest thinking level, which is again the company's own figure." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Transluce trains a separate observer role inside a model: 0.907 mean AUROC catching misbehaviour against 0.828 for transcript monitoring",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "On to the research, and a result that speaks directly to something we covered yesterday." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "In a paper on arXiv, authors from Transluce, MATS and Thinking Machines Lab trained a separate observer role inside a model, whose job is to answer questions about what the assistant just did." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And how well does that work?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "On five held-out oversight evaluations, the observer reaches a mean AUROC of 0.907. Reading the transcript from outside scores 0.828. Honesty training scores 0.864." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It also makes the assistant easier to watch by other methods. Follow-up questioning goes from 0.776 to 0.892." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The caveats matter. This is a preprint with no independent replication, and the headline numbers come from a single open-weight base model." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And one result cuts the other way. Reinforcement learning with reasoning raised the observer only from 0.719 to 0.823, below its best non-reasoning score." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Paper finds filtering training data only delays a capability, which returns with a fraction of the original data",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's one that challenges data filtering as a standing safeguard against dangerous capabilities. The idea is simple. If you don't want a model to have a capability, leave the data out of training." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The paper, on arXiv, withheld the training signal for individual skills across chess, Othello, molecular strings, bracket languages and web text, then put the data back later." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the models pick the skill back up with a small fraction of what they would have needed from scratch. A programming language withheld from a 7-billion-parameter model's training text came back with up to 13% of the model's training tokens." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The authors' own conclusion is, quote, data filtering therefore delays a capability. And the data needed to regain it falls as models get better." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Caveats. This is a preprint, no institution is printed on it, there's no independent replication, and it's the only source for the claim." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Which brings us to the security beat, where it was a heavy day." }
      ]
    },
