        { "host": "B", "text": "In a paper on arXiv, authors from Transluce, MATS and Thinking Machines Lab trained a separate observer role inside a model, whose job is to answer questions about what the assistant just did." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And how well does that work?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "On five held-out oversight evaluations, the observer reaches a mean AUROC of 0.907. Reading the transcript from outside scores 0.828. Honesty training scores 0.864." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It also makes the assistant easier to watch by other methods. Follow-up questioning goes from 0.776 to 0.892." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The caveats matter. This is a preprint with no independent replication, and the headline numbers come from a single open-weight base model." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And one result cuts the other way. Reinforcement learning with reasoning raised the observer only from 0.719 to 0.823, below its best non-reasoning score." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Paper finds filtering training data only delays a capability, which returns with a fraction of the original data",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's one that challenges data filtering as a standing safeguard against dangerous capabilities. The idea is simple. If you don't want a model to have a capability, leave the data out of training." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The paper, on arXiv, withheld the training signal for individual skills across chess, Othello, molecular strings, bracket languages and web text, then put the data back later." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the models pick the skill back up with a small fraction of what they would have needed from scratch. A programming language withheld from a 7-billion-parameter model's training text came back with up to 13% of the model's training tokens." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The authors' own conclusion is, quote, data filtering therefore delays a capability. And the data needed to regain it falls as models get better." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Caveats. This is a preprint, no institution is printed on it, there's no independent replication, and it's the only source for the claim." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Which brings us to the security beat, where it was a heavy day." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "GAO: covert testing got Claude, Grok, ChatGPT and Gemini to produce viable attack plans in under 12 hours each",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Government Accountability Office publicly released a report on November 18th. It was published back on October 16th." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "GAO says that in spring 2026 its prompts to Claude, Grok, ChatGPT and Gemini produced, quote, feasible information that could assist a malicious actor with planning and executing attacks." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What kind of attacks?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "GAO says two of the platforms produced information for constructing and detonating an improvised explosive device, and two produced information for attacking the electrical grid, one with a drone and one with firearms." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And it says each platform took less than 12 hours. Safeguards did fire in every case, denials, removed content, account suspensions, but GAO says it got around them using common bypass techniques." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Nextgov quotes GAO's Kevin Walsh saying, we got the nefarious information that we were after. And, these products are not fully in the control of the companies that are operating them." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "GAO made no recommendations, because it was examining third-party products. And it cautions this is a point-in-time assessment, not a statistically significant sample." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It also withheld the bypass techniques and which model did what, so nobody outside GAO can reproduce it from the report." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Security, misuse & threat intelligence",
      "headline": "F5 patches 5 critical NGINX flaws at CVSS 9.2; Xint's automated harness found 4 of the 5 unaided",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "And then the other side of the same coin. F5 patched a set of critical NGINX flaws, all rated CVSS 9.2." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "CyberScoop reports the security firm Xint found 4 of the 5 using an automated testing harness running with no human guidance." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "How fast?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Pointed at roughly 50,000 lines of NGINX source, it produced high-severity findings in about 90 minutes, and a working exploit about 10 minutes after that." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Register says these bugs were uncovered by researchers using AI, and that the firms that found them did so, quote, most, if not all, with an AI assist. It cites Netcraft's figure of around 225 million websites running NGINX." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Proof-of-concept code is already public. F5 does not report any exploitation in the wild, and says code execution requires ASLR to be disabled or bypassed." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Though Wiz says two of the bugs can themselves bypass it. And the timings are Xint's own figures, a company claim, not independently verified." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Now to export controls, where a rule became real." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "Commerce rule restricting Chinese firms' remote access to advanced chips takes effect; TSMC and Samsung reported to pause some China shipments",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The Bureau of Industry and Security rule on remote access to advanced semiconductors was published in the Federal Register and, in its own words, is effective November 19th." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "It restricts exports, reexports and in-country transfers to overseas data centres that remotely supply significant volumes of compute to companies headquartered in what the rule calls Country Group D:5, or Macau." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And it adds a licence requirement for exports of advanced semiconductors to Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Brazil, plus any further country the Secretary of Commerce designates." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It also formally rescinds the regulations implementing the Biden-era AI Diffusion Rule, which the notice says has never been enforced." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And there's an immediate consequence. Newsquawk, reporting the Financial Times, says TSMC and Samsung Electronics have suspended shipments of certain chips to Chinese customers while they seek US export licences." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Two caveats. This is an update, the rule's substance was announced on November 16th, so what's new is publication and the rule taking effect." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the shipment pause rests on a single source, the Financial Times. We could not open that article, so there's no detail on which products, which customers, or for how long." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "US-China commission asks Congress to bar Chinese open-weight models from critical infrastructure and national security systems",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The USCC, the US-China Economic and Security Review Commission, released its annual report to Congress, and the AI chapter carries several recommendations." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "One asks Congress to require Commerce, within 180 days, to identify sensitive uses, systems controlling critical infrastructure, performing national security functions, or processing bulk sensitive personal data." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And then to prohibit new deployment of covered models in those uses, absent a time-limited authorisation based on independently evaluated safeguards." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Another would require federal agencies, and anyone taking federal funds or tax credits, to disclose use of AI models developed by Chinese entities, including models fine-tuned on or derived from them, wherever they are hosted." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And businesses would have to disclose such use as a material business risk in their SEC filings." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The important caveat is that these are recommendations. The Commission has no rulemaking power, and Congress has historically adopted its recommendations selectively." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "To health, and a study about checking the models we already use in hospitals." }
      ]
    },
